# givn-spec

**Dialog policy:** for every user-facing question, follow the effective policy from `givn instructions dialog`; load it before this context's first question.

Write the Gherkin spec delta for change `{{CHANGE_ID}}`.

## Context

- Delta spec location: `givn/changes/{{CHANGE_ID}}/specs/<usecase-id>/<capability>.feature`
- Instructions: run `givn instructions specs --change {{CHANGE_ID}}`
- Proposal (WHY): `givn/changes/{{CHANGE_ID}}/proposal.md`
- Permanent specs: `givn/specs/`

## Use-case context

Before writing a delta, read the owning `givn/specs/<usecase-id>/usecase.md`
or the ideation change seed that names it. Preserve its goal, guarantees,
rules, typed relationships, Persona references, and capability interaction
inventory. Gherkin names domain actors and observable actions; it never embeds
Persona biographies. If this is a new capability, create or update the
complete change-side `usecase.md` under the use-case ID selected by routing.

## Delta tags

| Tag | Scope | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `@givn.delta` | Feature | Marks this as a delta document |
| `@<capability>` | Feature | Capability identifier (kebab-case) |
| `@givn.added` | Scenario | Append to permanent spec (default) |
| `@givn.modified` | Scenario | Replace scenario by title |
| `@givn.removed` | Scenario | Delete scenario by title |
| `@wip` | Scenario | The bound target is not yet implemented |

## Binding tags

Every scenario carries exactly one binding tag naming the native target that
proves it: `@check:<name>` (a flake check), `@host:<name>` (a host
configuration evaluation), or `@deploy:<name>` (a deploy test). `@e2e`
scenarios MUST bind to `@check:integration-*`, `@host:<name>`, or
`@deploy:<name>`. `docs/GIVN-VERIFICATION.md` is the canonical source.

## Rules

- Scenarios assert observable host/system state in domain language
  (Given/When/Then): a service active and listening, a firewall decision, a
  file/user/group/systemd unit, a materialised secret, or evaluation/boot
  state. No module paths, option names, helper functions, or target mechanics.
- One scenario = one observable behaviour.
- `@givn.removed` scenarios include exactly one placeholder step.
- On archive, all `@givn.*` tags are stripped automatically.
  The `@e2e` tag is preserved (it is not a `@givn.*` tag).

## Canonical verification policy

Before writing any scenario, read:

```sh
givn instructions specs --change {{CHANGE_ID}}
```

and `docs/GIVN-VERIFICATION.md`. They are the normative source for the binding
tags, the runners, and the targeted run commands. This skill only choreographs
when to read them and how to write the delta; it does not restate the policy.

